WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.142
This slide gives you a small completion exercise.

00:00:03.142 --> 00:00:07.732
The query should return active patients who are age fifty or older.

00:00:07.732 --> 00:00:10.417
One comparison operator is missing.

00:00:10.417 --> 00:00:15.042
Before typing anything, translate the requirement into plain language.

00:00:15.042 --> 00:00:21.207
The age condition needs to include patients who are exactly fifty as well as those who are older.

00:00:21.207 --> 00:00:26.662
Replace the placeholder with the operator that expresses that idea, then run the query.

00:00:26.662 --> 00:00:31.562
Look at the result and verify that the boundary value would be handled correctly.

00:00:31.562 --> 00:00:34.357
This exercise is intentionally small.

00:00:34.357 --> 00:00:38.837
The goal is to connect one phrase in the requirement to one SQL operator,

00:00:38.837 --> 00:00:41.747
not to solve a long query all at once.

00:00:41.747 --> 00:00:47.317
You can use the browser editor and Run, then compare your output with the expected meaning.

00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:52.252
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:58.532
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:58.532 --> 00:01:01.002
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:01.002 --> 00:01:06.527
In a real setting such as an application safely inserting or updating business records,

00:01:06.527 --> 00:01:12.682
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:12.682 --> 00:01:14.942
before discussing syntax.

00:01:14.942 --> 00:01:21.323
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
